{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1788878129246-architect-v2-memory-log-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '117,230p'\nnl -ba test/dao/stream/conformance.cljc | sed -n '459,565p'\nrg -n \\\"position.*tail|>.*tail|invalid-cursor|blocked\\\" test/dao/stream/conformance.cljc docs/design/dao.stream.md src/cljc/dao/stream -g '*.cljc'\"","aggregated_output":"   117\t## 3. Unbounded, `full`, and `transport-error`\n   118\t\n   119\t**Decision: logically unbounded; `full` and `transport-error` are both excluded.** The decision is what the review confirmed; the reasoning below is the corrected one.\n   120\t\n   121\t### `transport-error` (P1-1)\n   122\t\n   123\tThe line is not between the transport's medium and the host's. The vector does live on the host heap, and pretending otherwise was the weak part of r1. The line the contract now draws is between **a failure an operation can observe and return from** and **fatal host or runtime exhaustion from which the operation produces no result at all** — the first is inside the outcome algebra, the second is outside it entirely (`dao.stream.md:729-737`, amended in the working tree).\n   124\t\n   125\tEvery operation of this transport sits on one side of that line. Each is a single state transition over in-memory values — one `swap!`, one deref, one indexed lookup — and there is no intermediate condition it could detect and report: it completes and returns a result, or it does not return. That is the contract's own stated test for excluding the outcome (\"a transport whose every operation either completes its state transition or does not return therefore has no firing condition for `transport-error`\"), and it holds identically on clj, cljs and cljd, which is what an exclusion must do.\n   126\t\n   127\tNor is heap exhaustion ever converted into eviction: there is no code path in this namespace that removes an element (§5), so the contract's \"never … the eviction of acknowledged history\" is structurally satisfied rather than promised.\n   128\t\n   129\t### `full` (P2-1)\n   130\t\n   131\tA finite complete-history log returning **permanent** `full` is perfectly legitimate — the contract says `full` may be transient or permanent, and the writer decides what to do with any non-`ok` outcome. The r1 objection (\"retry never comes\") was therefore not a valid objection, and is withdrawn.\n   132\t\n   133\tThe actual reason to exclude it is a migration reason. v1's capacity-less local ring buffer never refused an append, so declaring a capacity here would introduce a write failure `dao.space` has never seen and has no policy for: the transactor would have to abort a transaction or roll the log over, and neither policy exists (see below). A migration that changes what `append!` can answer is not a migration. This transport declares no capacity, so it refuses nothing, and the contract permits the exclusion on exactly that basis (`dao.stream.md:727-729`).\n   134\t\n   135\t### What happens when the heap really is exhausted, and what is not available (P2-2)\n   136\t\n   137\tNothing in this transport, and — stated plainly rather than delegated upward — **nothing in the composition either, today.** r1 offered \"publish a checkpoint and start a fresh stream\" as the place a policy could live. That is not available:\n   138\t\n   139\t- A new transactor over an empty log derives `t = 0` and collides with the prior history's transaction numbers.\n   140\t- Publishing only the new log omits everything before the rotation.\n   141\t- Queries would need a snapshot chain or a merge policy across rotations, and neither exists.\n   142\t\n   143\tRotation is future work and requires a checkpoint that carries causality forward, plus an index and query composition designed around it. Until then, unbounded growth over a long-lived process is a **known, named limitation of this migration**, inherited unchanged from v1 — v1's capacity-less ring buffer has exactly the same property — and not something this transport either introduces or can fix.\n   144\t\n   145\tNo transport-owned size or depth operation is added. The contract permits one (`dao.stream.md:400-405`) and it would be the first thing contract-generic code reached for.\n   146\t\n   147\t## 4. Conformance\n   148\t\n   149\t### What the existing suite covers, and what changes\n   150\t\n   151\tThe manifest passes `validate-manifest`: `produces ∪ exclusions` equals `v2/operation-outcomes` per declared op, the sets are disjoint, every exclusion has a non-empty reason.\n   152\t\n   153\tThree law blocks behave differently from the ring buffer's run:\n   154\t\n   155\t- `run-reader-laws` (`conformance.cljc:171`) mints `:oldest` on a fresh handle and reads it twice. Here `:oldest` is position 0 and stays there forever, so the block passes for a stronger reason than on the ring buffer, where the anchor tracks an advancing watermark.\n   156\t- `run-induction-coverage` (`:112`) needs no `gap` inducer, since `gap` is excluded — the ring buffer's `gap` fixture (`ringbuffer_test.cljc:134-138`) has no counterpart and must not be reproduced by contrivance.\n   157\t- `run-close-laws` (`:231`) closes the logical stream, not an attachment.\n   158\t\n   159\t**The abstract model needs no change.** `make-abstract-stream-model` (`:382`) already takes `capacity nil` and guards its trim with `(and (:capacity state) …)` (`:481-485`). With no attachments, `visible-tail` is `:next-pos` and `retained-first` is 0 once anything is appended (`:next-pos`, also 0, when empty), so the `:oldest` expectation `(min (retained-first state) (visible-tail state inv))` (`:509`) is 0 at every state — the contract's \"a fresh `:oldest` *is* the origin\", already encoded. Supply a `:cursor-projector` for this transport's cursor keys and `capacity nil`; nothing else.\n   160\t\n   161\tLinearizability histories, mirroring `bounded-concurrent-linearizability-histories` (`ringbuffer_test.cljc:299`): concurrent append/append, append/next, append/close, and two cursors at position 0 read concurrently after one completed append (reader independence, here through two cursors on the owner handle rather than two attachments). `run-concurrently` is private to `ringbuffer_test`; duplicate the twelve-line helper rather than promote it — consolidating the copies is a separate cleanup.\n   162\t\n   163\t### Why an addition is needed at all\n   164\t\n   165\t`run-induction-coverage` verifies that every **declared** outcome is inducible, and its \"no observed outcome falls outside `:produces`\" check only inspects results from fixtures the manifest itself supplies. **No fixture can be written for an outcome the manifest excludes**, so an exclusion is today an unchecked assertion, and nothing in the harness can catch a transport that declares complete retention and evicts.\n   166\t\n   167\t\"Never evicts\" is a negative over unboundedly many appends and is not testable in general. What is testable is the observation a kept origin cursor makes, which is the mechanism the whole *Complete history* section rests on.\n   168\t\n   169\t### `run-retention-laws` (P1-3)\n   170\t\n   171\tAdded to `conformance.cljc` and run from `run-conformance-suite`. It runs **only when the manifest carries a `:retention` key**, so every existing manifest is unaffected.\n   172\t\n   173\t**Declaration checks**, before any operation:\n   174\t\n   175\t- `:retention` must be a known value — currently `#{:complete}`. An unrecognized value is a violation (`:unknown-retention-declaration`), never a silent skip, so a typo cannot disable the laws.\n   176\t- `:complete` requires the `:reader` surface (`:complete-retention-without-reader`). Completeness is a promise to a cursor; a transport with no reader has no one to make it to.\n   177\t- `:complete` requires `:next` to exclude `gap` (`:complete-retention-declares-gap`). The two declarations may not disagree.\n   178\t\n   179\t**Population.** The law needs a populated history and a cursor kept from before it existed:\n   180\t\n   181\t- If `:writer` is declared: mint `:dao.stream/oldest` on a fresh `:handle-factory` handle **first**, then append 256 distinct values. The cursor is a true pre-mutation origin cursor, which is the whole point.\n   182\t- If `:writer` is not declared — the contract explicitly permits a reader-only complete-history transport, \"a finite immutable history is a valid one\" (`dao.stream.md:722-724`) — the manifest must supply `:retention-fixture`, a thunk returning `{:handle h :expected [v …]}` for a prepopulated handle. Its absence is a violation (`:missing-retention-fixture`), not a pass. A reader-only manifest therefore cannot receive a vacuous certification, and the shape the contract blesses is not banned.\n   183\t\n   184\t**Replay checks.** A bounded helper follows a cursor to its terminus, collecting values and the set of outcomes seen, and reporting `:did-not-terminate` past `(+ (count expected) 8)` iterations. Each check asserts three things and names them separately:\n   185\t\n   186\t- no outcome in the replay was `gap` → `:gap-on-complete-retention`\n   187\t- the collected values equal `expected` → `:incomplete-replay`\n   188\t- the terminal outcome is the expected one → `:unexpected-terminal-outcome`\n   189\t\n   190\t**Comparing observations, not representations (P1-3.2).** The shared law never compares cursors structurally. It replays the kept origin cursor and, separately, a freshly minted `:oldest`, and requires the two **observed sequences** to be equal to `expected`. Structural cursor equality is asserted only in the memory-log-specific test, where the representation is the transport's own.\n   191\t\n   192\t**Including close (P1-3.3).** Where `:closable` is declared, the law then closes and repeats both replays, now expecting terminal `end` rather than `blocked`. Completeness lasts the logical stream's lifetime, and the existing `run-close-laws` only closes a fresh empty instance. If post-close `cursor` answers `closed` — a transport whose handles are attachments rather than owners — the fresh-`:oldest` half is skipped as inapplicable while the kept-origin-cursor half still runs, so nothing is waived.\n   193\t\n   194\tSketch of the core, to pin the shape:\n   195\t\n   196\t```clojure\n   197\t(def ^:private retention-declarations #{:complete})\n   198\t\n   199\t(defn- replay-from\n   200\t  \"Follow a cursor to its terminus, bounded.  Observations only.\"\n   201\t  [handle cursor limit]\n   202\t  (loop [c cursor n 0 vs [] seen #{}]\n   203\t    (if (> n limit)\n   204\t      {:values vs :terminal ::did-not-terminate :outcomes seen}\n   205\t      (let [r (v2/next handle c)\n   206\t            o (:dao.stream/outcome r)]\n   207\t        (if (= o :dao.stream/ok)\n   208\t          (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r) (inc n) (conj vs (:dao.stream/value r)) (conj seen o))\n   209\t          {:values vs :terminal o :outcomes (conj seen o)})))))\n   210\t```\n   211\t\n   212\t### The falsification test, committed (P1-3.1)\n   213\t\n   214\t`test/dao/stream/retention_falsification_test.cljc` — a dedicated namespace, because this is a test of the harness, not of either transport.\n   215\t\n   216\tThe adversary must be a manifest that **lies**, not one that is merely inconsistent: if it declared `gap` in `:produces`, the declaration check would fire and the behavioural replay would never run. So the test builds, from the ring buffer's real manifest, a manifest with `:retention :complete` **and** `:next` moved to exclude `gap` with the reason `\"false declaration under test\"`. Every declaration check then passes and only the replay can catch it. Against a capacity-2 ring buffer, the kept pre-eviction origin cursor's first `next` answers `gap`.\n   217\t\n   218\tThe test asserts `(false? (:passed? result))` and that some failure carries `:check :gap-on-complete-retention` — the specific P0 mechanism, observed through a cursor held from before the eviction, not merely \"something failed\". Removing the unused `gap` fixture entry is unnecessary: induction only looks up outcomes in `:produces`.\n   219\t\n   220\t### The transport's own tests (`memory_log_test.cljc`)\n   221\t\n   222\t- `complete-retention`: origin cursor, 1000 appends, full replay with no `gap`; a freshly minted `:oldest` is **structurally equal** to the origin cursor (permitted here, where the representation is ours); the same after `close!`, terminating in `end`.\n   223\t- `next-is-total` (P1-2): a fabricated cursor with the right identity and position `-1` → `invalid-cursor`; position `(inc tail)` → `invalid-cursor`; position `tail` → `blocked` open, `end` closed; a non-integer position → `invalid-cursor`; `nil` → `invalid-cursor`; a foreign identity → `cursor-mismatch`. All three hosts — this is a `.cljc` test with no host conditional, so all three run it.\n   224\t- `creation-spec-rule`: non-map, missing `:dao.stream/type`, wrong type, and a `:dao.stream.memory-log/…` key each → `invalid-spec`; a foreign qualified key (`:my.host/label`) → `ok`.\n   225\t- Conformance suite and linearizability histories as above.\n   226\t\n   227\t## 5. Cursors and reading\n   228\t\n   229\tState is the minimum that can express this transport:\n   230\t\n   459\t(defn abstract-stream-step\n   460\t  \"Pure abstract transition function for stream operations:\n   461\t   state: abstract model state\n   462\t   op: :append!, :close!, :cursor, or :next\n   463\t   args: arguments passed to operation\n   464\t   result: observed outcome map from operation\n   465\t   Returns next state if the operation and outcome are legally accepted by\n   466\t   the abstract sequential model; returns nil if transition is invalid.\"\n   467\t  [state op args result]\n   468\t  (let [outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n   469\t        inv (invocation args)\n   470\t        positional (:dao.stream/args inv)\n   471\t        attachment (attachment-state state inv)\n   472\t        handle-closed? (and attachment (not (:open? attachment)))]\n   473\t    (case op\n   474\t      :append!\n   475\t      (let [[_val] positional]\n   476\t        (if (or (:closed? state) handle-closed?)\n   477\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/closed) state)\n   478\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   479\t            (let [pos (:next-pos state)\n   480\t                  new-elem {:pos pos :val (first positional)}\n   481\t                  new-elements (conj (:elements state) new-elem)\n   482\t                  trimmed-elements (if (and (:capacity state)\n   483\t                                            (> (count new-elements) (:capacity state)))\n   484\t                                     (subvec new-elements (- (count new-elements) (:capacity state)))\n   485\t                                     new-elements)]\n   486\t              (assoc state\n   487\t                     :elements trimmed-elements\n   488\t                     :next-pos (inc pos))))))\n   489\t\n   490\t      :close!\n   491\t      (when (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   492\t        (if-let [id (attachment-id inv)]\n   493\t          (if (get-in state [:attachments id])\n   494\t            (if (get-in state [:attachments id :open?])\n   495\t              (assoc-in state [:attachments id]\n   496\t                        {:open? false :tail (:next-pos state)})\n   497\t              state)\n   498\t            nil)\n   499\t          (assoc state :closed? true)))\n   500\t\n   501\t      :cursor\n   502\t      (let [[anchor] positional]\n   503\t        (if handle-closed?\n   504\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/closed) state)\n   505\t          (case anchor\n   506\t            :dao.stream/oldest\n   507\t            (when (and (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   508\t                       (map? (:dao.stream/cursor result)))\n   509\t              (let [expected-pos (min (retained-first state) (visible-tail state inv))]\n   510\t                (when (= (result-cursor-position state result) expected-pos) state)))\n   511\t\n   512\t            :dao.stream/newest\n   513\t            (when (and (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   514\t                       (map? (:dao.stream/cursor result)))\n   515\t              (when (= (result-cursor-position state result) (visible-tail state inv)) state))\n   516\t\n   517\t            ;; Invalid anchor\n   518\t            (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-anchor)\n   519\t              state))))\n   520\t\n   521\t      :next\n   522\t      (let [[cur] positional]\n   523\t        (cond\n   524\t          (not (map? cur))\n   525\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-cursor) state)\n   526\t\n   527\t          (nil? (cursor-identity state cur))\n   528\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-cursor) state)\n   529\t\n   530\t          (not= (:stream-identity state) (cursor-identity state cur))\n   531\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch) state)\n   532\t\n   533\t          (not (integer? (cursor-position state cur)))\n   534\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-cursor) state)\n   535\t\n   536\t          :else\n   537\t          (let [pos (cursor-position state cur)\n   538\t                first-pos (retained-first state)\n   539\t                tail (visible-tail state inv)]\n   540\t            (cond\n   541\t              ;; Evicted position -> gap. Recovery names the earliest retained\n   542\t              ;; position, even when a closed attachment's frozen tail is gone.\n   543\t              (< pos first-pos)\n   544\t              (when (and (= outcome :dao.stream/gap)\n   545\t                         (= first-pos (result-cursor-position state result)))\n   546\t                state)\n   547\t\n   548\t              ;; Retained position visible through this handle -> ok.\n   549\t              (< pos tail)\n   550\t              (let [elem (first (filter #(= (:pos %) pos) (:elements state)))]\n   551\t                (when (and elem (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   552\t                           (= (:val elem) (:dao.stream/value result))\n   553\t                           (= (inc pos) (result-cursor-position state result)))\n   554\t                  state))\n   555\t\n   556\t              ;; A frozen attachment is at end even while the logical stream\n   557\t              ;; remains open.  Otherwise logical close determines end/blocked.\n   558\t              :else\n   559\t              (if (or handle-closed? (:closed? state))\n   560\t                (when (= outcome :dao.stream/end) state)\n   561\t                (when (= outcome :dao.stream/blocked) state))))))\n   562\t\n   563\t      ;; Unknown op\n   564\t      nil)))\ntest/dao/stream/conformance.cljc:515:              (when (= (result-cursor-position state result) (visible-tail state inv)) state))\ntest/dao/stream/conformance.cljc:525:          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-cursor) state)\ntest/dao/stream/conformance.cljc:528:          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-cursor) state)\ntest/dao/stream/conformance.cljc:534:          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-cursor) state)\ntest/dao/stream/conformance.cljc:542:              ;; position, even when a closed attachment's frozen tail is gone.\ntest/dao/stream/conformance.cljc:557:              ;; remains open.  Otherwise logical close determines end/blocked.\ntest/dao/stream/conformance.cljc:561:                (when (= outcome :dao.stream/blocked) state))))))\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:115:  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:147:has a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:189:`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:541:| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:542:| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:543:| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:545:| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:620:  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:640:`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/serving.cljc:303:      :dao.stream/blocked nil\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc:40:      :dao.stream/invalid-cursor :source-invalid-cursor\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc:86:  source `blocked` and destination `full`, and neither advances the cursor.\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc:118:               ;; Source `blocked` and destination `full` are the same answer\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc:72:   | `:retry`   | read blocked, or effect full            | unchanged | `:outcome`   |\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc:75:   | `:defect`  | read cursor-mismatch, invalid-cursor,\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc:107:      :dao.stream/blocked {:status :retry\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:402:      :dao.stream/blocked\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:413:      (:dao.stream/end :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:429:   A `:dao.stream/blocked` result yields immediately; this function never loops\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:68:                                     :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (:tail s)})\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:76:        (not (map? cursor-value)) (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:78:        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:82:        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:93:                    ;; position, which equals the tail when nothing is retained.\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:107:            :else (result :dao.stream/blocked))))))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:127:                   (-> s (assoc :tail n :first first')\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:337:        :dao.stream/blocked\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:350:        (:dao.stream/end :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
